      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1206,
      "output": "Water is a chemical substance that is essential for life on Earth. Here is a breakdown of what it is, how it is made, and why it is important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Composition\nChemically, water is known by the formula **$H_2O$**. This means that every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are bonded together by a covalent bond.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter at standard temperatures and pressures:\n*   **Solid:** As ice (which floats on liquid water).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we are most familiar with.\n*   **Gas:** As water vapor (invisible in the air).\n\n### 3. Polarity\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen atom pulls electrons toward itself, giving the oxygen a slight negative charge and the hydrogen atoms a slight positive charge. This polarity is what makes water such a powerful solvent (it can dissolve many other substances) and allows it to stick to itself (cohesion).\n\n### 4. Key Properties\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is the main transport system in the human body (carrying nutrients and waste) and in the environment (moving nutrients through soil).\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it gets cold. This helps regulate the Earth's temperature and keeps the human body stable.\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots.\n\n### 5. Importance to Life\nWater is the foundation of life. It is a major component of all living things (the human body is about 60% water) and is necessary for:\n*   Digestion and nutrient absorption.\n*   Temperature regulation (sweating).\n*   Lubricating joints and organs.\n*   Removing waste through urination and sweating."
